  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Economic characteristics and the likelihood of first birth, <span class="refper">2017 to 2023</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260909/dq260909a-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260909/dq260909a-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-09-09T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Canada's declining fertility has led to increased interest in the factors that influence the decision to have children. A new study released today examines how income and employment characteristics relate to the likelihood of having a first child.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Stocks of principal field crops, <span class="refper">July 31, 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260909/dq260909b-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260909/dq260909b-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-09-09T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Total stocks for wheat, canola, dry peas, lentils and oats were up as of July 31, while stocks of barley were down compared with the same date one year earlier. </div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Quarterly rent statistics, <span class="refper">second quarter 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260909/dq260909c-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260909/dq260909c-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-09-09T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">The average asking rent for a two-bedroom apartment across all census metropolitan areas&#160;(CMAs) combined was $2,130&#160;per month in the second&#160;quarter of&#160;2026, down 3.6% from the second&#160;quarter of&#160;2025. </div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Retail Commodity Survey, <span class="refper">June 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260908/dq260908a-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260908/dq260908a-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-09-08T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Retail sales reached $78.4&#160;billion in June, an increase of 7.2% compared with the same month one year earlier. Higher sales were reported in 17&#160;of the 18&#160;commodity classes.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">New motor vehicle registrations, <span class="refper">second quarter 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260908/dq260908b-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260908/dq260908b-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-09-08T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Canadians registered 547,673&#160;new motor vehicles in the second quarter, the highest level since the second quarter of 2019. In the second quarter of 2026, new motor vehicle registrations were up 1.1% from the second quarter of 2025 and 37.7% from the first quarter of 2026.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
