  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Labour Force Survey, <span class="refper">August 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260904/dq260904a-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260904/dq260904a-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-09-04T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Employment declined by 42,000 (-0.2%) in August and the employment rate fell 0.1 percentage points to 60.8%. The unemployment rate was unchanged at 6.4%.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Sawmills, <span class="refper">June 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260904/dq260904b-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260904/dq260904b-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-09-04T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Lumber production increased 0.2% from May to 3&#160;894.4&#160;thousand cubic metres in June. Production was down 1.9% from June&#160;2025. </div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Farm Product Price Index, <span class="refper">June 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260904/dq260904c-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260904/dq260904c-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-09-04T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">The Farm Product Price Index rose 4.0% in June compared with the same month one year earlier. This increase was mainly due to the gain in the cattle and calves index (+17.5%), which more than offset the declines in the indexes of hogs (-6.4%) and total crops (-0.2%).</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Sector-based Producer Price Indexes: announcement</div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260904/dq260904d-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260904/dq260904d-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-09-04T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Today, Statistics Canada announces the creation of the Sector-based Producer Price Indexes. These indexes have been built to measure price changes across four producer sectors&#8212;energy, non-energy, goods and services&#8212;using a methodology developed by the Bank of Canada. The indexes will be published for the first time on October&#160;9,&#160;2026. These data support research on firms' price-setting behaviours and inflation dynamics, including the development of economic models.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Canadian international merchandise trade, <span class="refper">July 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260903/dq260903a-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260903/dq260903a-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-09-03T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">In July, Canada's merchandise exports decreased 2.3%, while imports were up 2.2%. As a result, Canada's merchandise trade surplus with the world narrowed from $4.2 billion in June to $769 million in July. This was the fifth consecutive monthly trade surplus.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Canadian international trade in services, <span class="refper">July 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260903/dq260903b-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260903/dq260903b-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-09-03T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">In July, Canada's exports of services increased 1.2% to reach $21.0 billion, while its imports of services declined 1.1% to $20.7 billion. As a result, Canada's monthly international trade in services balance went from a deficit of $0.2 billion in June to a surplus of $0.3 billion in July. </div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Labour productivity, hourly compensation and unit labour cost, <span class="refper">second quarter 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260903/dq260903c-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260903/dq260903c-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-09-03T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">The labour productivity of Canadian businesses increased 1.0% in the second quarter, the largest increase since the rise in the second quarter of 2020 (+7.7%), induced by the COVID-19 pandemic. The increase in the second quarter of 2026 resulted from a rise in the value added in the business sector, while hours worked decreased.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Study: Residential properties and homeowners in high flood hazard areas, <span class="refper">2022</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260903/dq260903d-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260903/dq260903d-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-09-03T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">The study "Analysis of residential properties and homeowners in high flood hazard areas," released today, uses housing data from the Canadian Housing Statistics Program and flood hazard data from Public Safety Canada to provide insight on the flood exposure of residential properties in Canadian communities.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
