  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Investment in building construction, <span class="refper">June 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260819/dq260819a-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260819/dq260819a-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-08-19T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">The total value of investment in building construction decreased $109.7&#160;million (-0.5%) to $23.2&#160;billion in June.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Health Reports, <span class="refper">August 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260819/dq260819b-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260819/dq260819b-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-08-19T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Environmental exposures such as air pollution, extreme heat and cold, and limited green space are known to affect health. When people move residences, their risk of exposure changes. Few national datasets have captured both exposures to multiple environments and residential mobility. The article entitled "Residential mobility and multi-domain environmental exposures in Canada: Development of the Residential Mobility Exposome File" introduces the Residential Mobility Exposome File (ReMEF) to fill this gap. The ReMEF linked residential historical postal codes to annual measures across three domains (ambient air pollution, built and green environment, and ambient temperature) using the Canadian Census Health and Environment Cohort for 1991, 1996 and 2001, with up to 25 years of follow-up.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Urban public transit, <span class="refper">June 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260819/dq260819c-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260819/dq260819c-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-08-19T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">In June, urban transit posted its first year-over-year ridership increase since April&#160;2025, reaching 129.0&#160;million passenger trips in June&#160;2026, 3.3% higher than in June&#160;2025.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
