  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Police-reported crime statistics in Canada, <span class="refper">2025</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260722/dq260722a-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260722/dq260722a-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-07-22T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">The Crime Severity Index (CSI), an established composite measure of the volume and severity of police-reported crime, decreased 5% from 2024 to 2025. This marked the second consecutive annual decrease, following an upward trend beginning in 2015. In 2025, both the violent CSI (-4%) and the non-violent CSI (-5%) decreased.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Economic and Social Reports, <span class="refper">July 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260722/dq260722b-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260722/dq260722b-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-07-22T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">There are five&#160;new articles in today's release of Economic and Social Reports.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Passenger bus and urban transit, <span class="refper">2024</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260722/dq260722c-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260722/dq260722c-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-07-22T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Total revenue for passenger bus and urban transit companies operating in Canada rose&#160;11.2% year over year to $33.9&#160;billion in 2024, with urban transit agencies accounting for more than three-quarters&#160;($2.6&#160;billion) of the increase. All segments of the passenger bus and urban transit sector continue to recover from the impacts of the COVID-19&#160;pandemic albeit at different rates.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Airport activity changes course in 2025</div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260721/dq260721a-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260721/dq260721a-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-07-21T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">In 2025, 158.9&#160;million passengers enplaned and deplaned at Canadian airports, up&#160;1.3% from 2024. This remained just below the 2019 level, prior to the COVID-19 pandemic, as year-over-year growth has slowed in recent years. Indeed, the number of passengers enplaned and deplaned at Canadian airports in 2025 represented 97.6% of the number recorded in 2019. </div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Urban public transit, <span class="refper">May 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260721/dq260721b-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260721/dq260721b-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-07-21T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">In May, Canada's urban transit systems logged just over 127&#160;million passenger trips, 4.8%&#160;lower than in May&#160;2025.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Consumer Price Index, <span class="refper">June 2026</span></div>
    </title>
    <link href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260720/dq260720a-eng.htm"></link>
    <id>https://www.statcan.gc.ca/daily-quotidien/260720/dq260720a-eng.htm</id>
    <updated>2026-07-20T08:30:00-04:00</updated>
    <summary type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">The Consumer Price Index (CPI) increased 2.8% year over year in June, following a 3.2% gain in May. On a seasonally adjusted monthly basis, the CPI fell 0.1% in June.</div>
    </summary>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title type="xhtml">
      <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Investment in building construction, <span class="refper">May 2026</span></div>
